rulings: ["[{'date': '2018-01-19', 'text': 'Augment can (and usually does) change the name, card types, subtypes, rules text, and power/toughness. The combined creature will have (at least) two artists and may now have multiple colors. Anything covered up in the augment process doesn’t count, so ignore things to the left of the “metal bar” in the art of host creatures.'}, {'date': '2018-01-19', 'text': 'Augment can’t target creatures that aren’t host creatures.'}, {'date': '2018-01-19', 'text': 'Augment is an activated ability that you activate from your hand. To do so, reveal the card, choose a target host creature, and pay the augment cost. As this ability resolves, if the card with augment is still in your hand, put it onto the battlefield combined with the host creature.'}, {'date': '2018-01-19', 'text': 'Creatures with augment don’t have a mana cost and can’t be cast.'}, {'date': '2018-01-19', 'text': 'The creature card with augment isn’t put onto the battlefield until the ability resolves. This means if the host is destroyed, the creature with augment stays in your hand. You can’t choose a different host, but you can activate augment again if there’s another host available.'}, {'date': '2018-01-19', 'text': 'You can’t activate augment unless there is a host creature on the battlefield. It doesn’t need to be yours. Note though that if you augment another player’s host creature, they control the combined creature.'}, {'date': '2018-01-19', 'text': 'You can’t put more than one augment card on a single host creature. Once a host creature is augmented, the host part gets covered up and it’s no longer a host creature.'}]", "[{'date': '2018-01-19', 'text': 'Host creatures each have an ability that triggers when it enters the battlefield. It functions like any other creature.'}, {'date': '2018-01-19', 'text': 'This card has received minor errata so you can’t flicker creatures more than once each turn. Oops.'}]", "[{'date': '2018-01-19', 'text': 'Host creatures each have an ability that triggers when it enters the battlefield. It functions like any other creature.'}]", "[{'date': '2018-01-19', 'text': 'If asked to choose a color in a silver-bordered game, you can choose gold.'}, {'date': '2018-01-19', 'text': 'The Dragon token is monocolored. It’s gold.'}]", '[{\'date\': \'2020-02-29\', \'text\': "If no artist is listed, no letters are noted. You won\'t put a +1/+1 counter on Abstract Iguanart."}, {\'date\': \'2020-02-29\', \'text\': "If the artist is credited in a language other than English, use the first character that appears (even if in that language, the character isn\'t a letter, strictly speaking)."}, {\'date\': \'2020-02-29\', \'text\': "If the card has multiple artists listed (usually separated by &), note the first letter of each of them. For example, if you cast a spell illustrated by Zoltan Boros & Gabor Sziksai, note the letters Z and G. If neither letter had previously been noted, you\'d put two +1/+1 counters on Abstract Iguanart."}, {\'date\': \'2020-02-29\', \'text\': "The first letter of the artist\'s name is the first letter of how that artist is credited. For example, the D in Deruchenko Alexander, the B in Brom, or the C in Claymore J. Flapdoodle."}]', '[{\'date\': \'2020-02-29\', \'text\': "If you cast a Squirrel spell that depicts a squirrel (as most do), you\'d get two acorn counters: one when you cast the spell, and another when the spell resolves and the Squirrel enters the battlefield."}, {\'date\': \'2020-02-29\', \'text\': \'You retain all acorn counters in your stash until you spend them, even if you lose control of Acornelia.\'}]', "[{'date': '2020-02-29', 'text': 'A hat is a garment worn on the head that’s not part of another garment. Hoodies, for example, are not hats. Items that only cover the face and not the top of the head, such as a face mask, are also not hats.'}, {'date': '2020-02-29', 'text': 'If the card represents multiple creatures and at least one of them is wearing a hat, the creature will have menace.'}, {'date': '2020-02-29', 'text': 'If the creature is a hat, it won’t have menace, unless it itself is wearing a hat.'}, {'date': '2020-02-29', 'text': 'The creature that the card represents must be wearing a hat to have menace. A background character or a scale bird wearing a hat wouldn’t count. (But scale birds in hats! Adorable!)'}]", "[{'date': '2020-02-29', 'text': 'A permanent card that becomes the top card of your library enters the battlefield (unless it went from the battlefield to the top of your library), so any enters-the-battlefield abilities will trigger and “as [this creature] enters the battlefield” abilities will apply. Similarly, a permanent card that stops being the top card of your library leaves the battlefield (unless it went from the top of your library to the battlefield directly).'}, {'date': '2020-02-29', 'text': 'If a creature you control that isn’t part of your library is put on top of your library, surprisingly little happens, at least at first. That creature doesn’t leave or enter the battlefield. It just stays on the battlefield. Of course, now that it’s also the top card of your library, you’ll probably draw it soon. This is also true if something puts the top card of your library directly onto the battlefield, without you having to play it.'}, {'date': '2020-02-29', 'text': 'If the top card is your library is an Aura card, that Aura is on the battlefield not attached to anything. As such, it’s put into your graveyard. The new top card of your library is then revealed.'}, {'date': '2020-02-29', 'text': 'If the top card of your library is a creature card and it’s put into your graveyard, the creature did die. Note that it wasn’t destroyed or sacrificed or anything. It just died. <sad trombone noises>'}, {'date': '2020-02-29', 'text': 'If the top card of your library is a creature card, it can attack. It does have haste, after all. It can also block.'}, {'date': '2020-02-29', 'text': 'If the top card of your library is a creature, planeswalker, land, enchantment, or artifact card, it’s also on the battlefield. Ta-da! This is, as they say, very weird.'}, {'date': '2020-02-29', 'text': 'If the top card of your library is a permanent card with an activated ability that requires it to be tapped, that ability can be activated. Land cards on top of your library can be tapped for mana, and so on.'}, {'date': '2020-02-29', 'text': 'If you are instructed to draw multiple cards, you draw them one at a time. Each card you draw after the first becomes the top card of your library briefly and is revealed. Each permanent card revealed this way enters and then leaves the battlefield, for any abilities that care about that.'}]", "[{'date': '2020-02-29', 'text': 'A permanent or card in another zone with no name (including face-down creatures, ____________, and ones whose name Stet has decided to fully delete) can’t begin with the same letter as anything else. If _______ is exiled this way, Stet won’t deal any damage. Similarly, Stet won’t deal damage to any creature with no name, no matter what card was exiled.'}, {'date': '2020-02-29', 'text': 'Use whatever name or names a player commonly goes by to determine what their current name is and if they should be dealt damage.'}]", "[{'date': '2020-02-29', 'text': 'Ability words themselves are not rules text, but they’re followed by rules text, so any line with an ability word will count.'}, {'date': '2020-02-29', 'text': 'Any line with at least one word of rules text counts as a line of rules text.'}, {'date': '2020-02-29', 'text': 'Its name is Alexander Clamilton.'}, {'date': '2020-02-29', 'text': 'Some card layouts include rules text in different sections. Count each one separately. For example, “the battlefield” in the “pin-up” section of a host creature is counted separately from the first line of text of that ability’s effect.'}, {'date': '2020-02-29', 'text': 'There are a million things it hasn’t done, including count reminder text and flavor text. Those aren’t rules text.'}, {'date': '2020-02-29', 'text': 'This card’s name is Alexander Clamilton.'}]", "[{'date': '2020-02-29', 'text': 'After you choose a card, all cards with that name are removed from the game in progress, no matter what zone they’re in. If there are any in a player’s sideboard, those are removed as well. Those cards remain out of the game for the rest of that game and for any subsequent game in that match.'}, {'date': '2020-02-29', 'text': 'If cards from a player’s main deck are banned, they must replace them with cards from their sideboard to begin the next game of the match. If enough cards are banned such that they can’t meet the deck minimum, they can begin the game with their main deck having fewer cards than is normally required. However, if they end up with fewer than seven cards available, they’ll lose the game as it begins when they are forced to try and draw from their suddenly empty library.'}, {'date': '2020-02-29', 'text': 'The DCI, originally short for Duelists’ Convocation International and later short for absolutely nothing, was the name given to the governing body overseeing all Magic organized play. Although this name has since been retired, the card Look at Me, I’m the DCI persists in its honor.'}, {'date': '2020-02-29', 'text': 'The ban doesn’t affect tokens, including ones that already exist when the ban goes into effect and future ones that may be created.'}]", "[{'date': '2020-02-29', 'text': 'All nine rows count. You can fill rows horizontally, vertically, and diagonally. A full card with give B-I-N-G-O +72/+72.'}, {'date': '2020-02-29', 'text': 'Casting multiple spells with the same mana value has no additional benefit. Once a spot has a chip counter, it’s marked, and additional chip counters won’t do anything.'}, {'date': '2020-02-29', 'text': 'The counters are considered to be on the inherent tracker in B-I-N-G-O’s art, not on the creature itself. Those counters can’t be removed or moved to another permanent. Similarly, if you proliferate, you can’t add more chip counters to B-I-N-G-O.'}]", "[{'date': '2020-02-29', 'text': 'Any even number of Topsy Turvys (Turvies?) will essentially cancel each other out and return the order of phases to their normal order.'}, {'date': '2020-02-29', 'text': 'Steps within each phase play out as normal. For example, although the beginning phase happens last in a turn, the untap, upkeep, and draw steps happen in their traditional order. (If it were the other way, combat would require psychics to get through.)'}]", "[{'date': '2020-02-29', 'text': 'Avatar of Me is only the color of your eyes, not the color of your eyes and blue. That is, the color it gets from its last ability overrides the blue it gets from its mana cost.'}, {'date': '2020-02-29', 'text': 'If each of your eyes is a different color (naturally or via a single contact lens), Avatar of Me is both colors.'}, {'date': '2020-02-29', 'text': 'If you’re wearing colored contact lenses, Avatar of Me is whatever color your eyes appear to be. You can add or remove contact lenses to change your eye color in response to spells and abilities.'}, {'date': '2020-02-29', 'text': 'Silver-bordered rules include any conceivable eye color, not just the five traditional Magic colors. If your eye are hazel, Avatar of Me is hazel.'}, {'date': '2020-02-29', 'text': 'Thanks to Avatar of Me, if you’re asked to choose a color in a silver-bordered game, you can name any color. “That dreamy shade of blue found only in the sky on a perfect afternoon… and in your eyes” is acceptable, but a little weird.'}]", "[{'date': '2020-02-29', 'text': 'B.O.B. is not a creature and is not itself a Beeble.'}, {'date': '2020-02-29', 'text': 'B.O.B.’s loyalty counts all Beebles you control, not just the tokens B.O.B. creates. Or separates? Whatever B.O.B. does.'}, {'date': '2020-02-29', 'text': 'Conversely, other cards like Primal Vigor don’t mention “effects.” If you control one of these cards, activating B.O.B.’s first loyalty ability would cause you to create two Beebles, and thus put two loyalty counters on B.O.B.'}, {'date': '2020-02-29', 'text': 'Every time B.O.B loses loyalty, you choose which Beebles to sacrifice. If B.O.B. is dealt more damage than the number of Beebles you control, you sacrifice all of them, and then B.O.B. is put into its owner’s graveyard.'}, {'date': '2020-02-29', 'text': 'If B.O.B. gains loyalty, the Beeble you create is a 1/1 blue Beeble creature token.'}, {'date': '2020-02-29', 'text': 'If you control Doubling Season or a similar card that applies “is an effect would create one or more tokens,” you’d create eight Beebles as B.O.B. enters the battlefield. However, because such cards apply only to effects and not costs, you’d still create only one Beeble if you activated B.O.B.’s first loyalty ability.'}, {'date': '2020-02-29', 'text': 'Sacrificing Beebles to account for B.O.B.’s lost loyalty happens before state-based actions clean up the lethally-damaged Beebles. The one that blocked can heroically be the one you sacrifice. Farewell, brave Beeble. B.O.B.-speed.'}]", "[{'date': '2020-02-29', 'text': 'By default, you are limited to cards legal in the format you’re playing. If you’re playing Draft or Sealed Deck, you can name any card available in the format you’re playing. If you’re playing with Unsanctioned decks, anything goes. Discuss with your playgroup if you’d prefer to modify or eliminate these limitations.'}, {'date': '2020-02-29', 'text': 'Each Richard tracks its own list of played cards. If one leaves the battlefield and another enters, cards previously played under the first Richard are available once again.'}, {'date': '2020-02-29', 'text': 'If more than one player controls a Richard Garfield, Ph.D., the cards one player plays has no effect on the other players. For example, each player could play Savannah Lions as a Swords to Plowshares this way.'}, {'date': '2020-02-29', 'text': 'Playing cards as though they were other cards is optional.'}, {'date': '2020-02-29', 'text': 'Richard Garfield, Ph.D. essentially makes you play a variant of the Magic game called “Mental Magic.” Here’s how the game works. Whenever you cast a spell, you reveal the card to all players and choose any other card (see below for the cards that you can use) that has that card’s mana cost. (Note that color very much does matter.) That spell can then be cast as though it was the chosen card. You aren’t allowed to choose the same card twice.'}, {'date': '2020-02-29', 'text': 'The same is true if Richard Garfield, Ph.D. changes controllers. If you play a Savannah Lions as Swords to Plowshares, then an opponent gains control of Richard Garfield, Ph.D., that player can then do the same.'}]", "[{'date': '2020-02-29', 'text': 'Cards with no mana costs (including cards with augment) have a converted mana cost of 0.'}, {'date': '2020-02-29', 'text': 'If you exile a card with augment but don’t control a host, the card with augment stays in exile. It won’t enter the battlefield.'}]", "[{'date': '2020-02-29', 'text': 'Cheatyface’s mana cost is {U}{U}{U}.'}, {'date': '2020-02-29', 'text': 'You must legally get Cheatyface into your hand before you can attempt to sneak it onto the battlefield. For example, you can’t sneak it in from your sideboard or collection during a game.'}]", "[{'date': '2020-02-29', 'text': 'Chicken à la King can be tapped to activate its last ability. That ability doesn’t use {T}, so any untapped Bird you control will do, even one that entered the battlefield that turn.'}, {'date': '2020-02-29', 'text': 'Like other Chickens, Chicken à la King has received errata so its creature type is Bird. Additionally, its abilities now refer to Birds.'}]", "[{'date': '2020-02-29', 'text': 'Die creatures have an inherent ability to be rerolled at any time, not just when they were initially rolled. Pippa’s second ability can reroll them, as well as effects of cards like Wall of Fortune.'}, {'date': '2020-02-29', 'text': 'For the first ability, you roll a die that isn’t already in the game. That die will be used to represent the Die creature you create.'}, {'date': '2020-02-29', 'text': 'Pippa’s second ability can be used to reroll any die roll, not just yours. If you reroll another player’s roll, the result still counts for whatever instructed them to roll a die, but abilities you control that trigger “whenever you roll a die” will trigger.'}, {'date': '2020-02-29', 'text': 'The Die creature you create enters the battlefield.'}]", "[{'date': '2020-02-29', 'text': 'Due to changes in how echo and phasing work since Old Fogey was originally printed, echo mostly does nothing in this case. This is, as they say, a bit weird. Old Fogey is phased out when its echo ability would trigger, so that ability doesn’t trigger at the beginning of the upkeep after Old Fogey enters the battlefield. Phasing in doesn’t cause Old Fogey to enter the battlefield, so echo ends up not really doing much at all.'}]", "[{'date': '2020-02-29', 'text': 'Duh can target a creature token only if the object you’re using to represent the token has reminder text, even if it’s a copy of a creature with reminder text.'}, {'date': '2020-02-29', 'text': 'Look at the actual creature you’re hoping to destroy to see if it has reminder text. Its Oracle text doesn’t matter for this purpose. It also doesn’t matter if another printing of the creature has (or doesn’t have) reminder text.'}]", "[{'date': '2020-02-29', 'text': 'Emcee’s ability will trigger whenever a creature enters the battlefield under an opponent’s control. You choose whether you announce its arrival or not.'}, {'date': '2020-02-29', 'text': 'If you are unable (not just unwilling) to use a deep, booming voice, you are free to make accommodations in the spirit of Emcee. Sign overly dramatically, for example.'}, {'date': '2020-02-29', 'text': 'If you control more than one Emcee, each ability triggers separately. You have to announce the creature’s entrance each time. Okay, you can just stay standing if you want.'}, {'date': '2020-02-29', 'text': 'You may add in nicknames for the creatures you announce, but you must also get the creature’s actual name in there to count. For example, “Gary, the Gray Merchant of Asphodel” is fine, but simply “Gary” wouldn’t work.'}]", "[{'date': '2020-02-29', 'text': 'Entirely Normal Armchair can be hidden in plain sight (i.e. just sitting there).'}, {'date': '2020-02-29', 'text': 'You must legally get Entirely Normal Armchair into your hand before you can attempt to sneak it onto the battlefield. For example, you can’t sneak it in from your sideboard or collection during a game.'}, {'date': '2020-02-29', 'text': 'Your opponent may activate Entirely Normal Armchair’s first activated ability even if they can’t literally see it, provided they can identify where it is. That is, you can’t just put your hand over it and claim that can’t see. Well, you can claim that, and it might be true, but it won’t do what you want it to.'}]", "[{'date': '2020-02-29', 'text': 'Flashback and buyback don’t mix. If you cast Blast from the Past using flashback, it will be exiled rather than go back to your hand.'}, {'date': '2020-02-29', 'text': 'Flashback and kicker do mix. You can pay the kicker cost in addition to the flashback cost to get the extra Goblin token.'}, {'date': '2020-02-29', 'text': 'If you cycle Blast from the Past, you can then use madness to cast it. If you cast Blast from the Past using madness, you may also pay for kicker or buyback.'}]", "[{'date': '2020-02-29', 'text': 'For determining which ability you can activate, use an official source that tracks when sunrise and sunset are in your area.'}, {'date': '2020-02-29', 'text': 'If a Knight you control has lifelink, Syr Cadian won’t cause you to gain additional life.'}, {'date': '2020-02-29', 'text': 'If it’s the exact minute of sunrise or sunset, you may activate both abilities. But hurry!'}, {'date': '2020-02-29', 'text': 'Knightlifelink is the same as granting lifelink to each Knight you control, but much, much cooler.'}, {'date': '2020-02-29', 'text': 'Syr Cadian is a Knight, so knightlifelink applies to it.'}]", "[{'date': '2020-02-29', 'text': 'Frankie Peanuts hates snitches, but he detests unsporting conduct. Keep your questions appropriate for your game and its surroundings.'}, {'date': '2020-02-29', 'text': 'If you’re the player answering the yes-or-no question, you must abide by your answer if able. For example, if you are asked “Is the Slaying Mantis you control going to attack on your next turn?” and you say yes, then it must attack if able. However, if it’s tapped as you declare attackers that turn, then you aren’t able to attack with it, and you don’t have to abide by your answer.'}]", "[{'date': '2020-02-29', 'text': 'If Boomstacker is a token, you must stack the dice on top of whatever object you’re using to represent the token.'}, {'date': '2020-02-29', 'text': 'The dice must be placed on top of Boomstacker.'}, {'date': '2020-02-29', 'text': 'The “stack falls” if one or more dice that are part of the stack fall from it. Note that a die isn’t part of the stack until it rests on top of the stack and you are no longer touching the die. This means that if you drop the die you’re trying to place before you’re done, the stack hasn’t fallen. YET.'}, {'date': '2020-02-29', 'text': 'You must still tap Boomstacker if it attacks or becomes tapped for some other reason. That’s the fun part.'}, {'date': '2020-02-29', 'text': 'You must use actual dice, although the dice don’t have to be six-sided. Using coins and calling them “two-sided dice” isn’t allowed. We recommend against using four-sided dice for this.'}]", "[{'date': '2020-02-29', 'text': 'If Enter the Dungeon is cast during a subgame, a sub-subgame begins, preferably under a new, smaller table, but do what you can. The winner of the sub-sub game would search their subgame library for two cards.'}]", "[{'date': '2020-02-29', 'text': 'If Slaying Mantis lands on multiple creatures, it fights each of them one-on-one. For example, Slaying Mantis lands on a 3/3, a 4/4, and a 5/5. Slaying Mantis deals 6 damage to each of them, destroying them all. At the same time, they deal a total of 12 damage to Slaying Mantis, destroying it. What a maneuver!'}, {'date': '2020-02-29', 'text': 'If Slaying Mantis touches the object used to represent a creature token an opponent controls, it will fight that creature.'}, {'date': '2020-02-29', 'text': 'If you create a token that’s a copy of Slaying Mantis, it also enters via flying elbow. However, you must use a Magic card-sized object to represent the token. As Slaying Mantis’s just a second ability won’t apply, players can move around their permanents at any time, including while Slaying Mantis is in flight. So that’s fun.'}, {'date': '2020-02-29', 'text': 'Just a second is a variant of split second. Announcing Slaying Mantis instantly locks all permanents in place on the battlefield. As long as Slaying Mantis is on the stack, players can’t cast spells, activate abilities, or move their permanents.'}, {'date': '2020-02-29', 'text': 'No player may interfere with the throwing of Slaying Mantis, including while it’s in flight.'}, {'date': '2020-02-29', 'text': 'Slaying Mantis must be thrown onto the battlefield from at least three feet away. That is, three feet is a horizonal distance, not a vertical one.'}, {'date': '2020-02-29', 'text': 'Slaying Mantis will fight any creature an opponent controls that it touched as it entered, even if it didn’t end up touching that creature when it stopped moving.'}]", "[{'date': '2020-02-29', 'text': 'If Super-Duper Death Ray doesn’t resolve because its target leaves the battlefield or otherwise becomes an illegal target in response, it will have no effect. It won’t deal damage to any creature or player.'}, {'date': '2020-02-29', 'text': 'If damage is already marked on the creature, that damage is considered when calculating excess damage. For example, say your opponent controls a 6/6 creature. Your first Super-Duper Death Ray deals 4 damage to it. Then your SECOND Super-Duper Death Ray (i.e. the one they never see coming) deals 2 damage to the creature and 2 damage to your opponent.'}, {'date': '2020-02-29', 'text': 'Super-Duper Death Ray targets only the creature. It can deal damage to a player with hexproof, for example.'}, {'date': '2020-02-29', 'text': 'Trample on an instant or sorcery is similar to how it works on an attacking creature. If Super-Duper Death Ray would deal more damage that what it would take to destroy the creature (called “excess damage”), it deals the excess to the creature’s controller. For example, if you aim Super-Duper Death Ray at a 1/1 creature, it will deal 1 damage to the creature and 3 damage to its controller.'}]", "[{'date': '2020-02-29', 'text': 'If a player rolls a die, you can tap an untapped Wall you control to have that player reroll that die. Then, if you didn’t like that result, you can tap another untapped Wall you control in order to have them reroll again.'}, {'date': '2020-02-29', 'text': 'The Wall you tap to use Wall of Fortune’s ability can be one that came under your control this turn. This includes Wall of Fortune itself.'}, {'date': '2020-02-29', 'text': 'You can use Wall of Fortune to have a player reroll any die, including the planar die or the d20 from Sword of Dungeons & Dragons.'}, {'date': '2020-02-29', 'text': 'You decide whether to use Wall of Fortune’s ability right after you see the result of the die roll.'}]", "[{'date': '2020-02-29', 'text': 'If an effect sets your life total to a specific number, it will be that number, even if you previously had infinite life.'}, {'date': '2020-02-29', 'text': 'If you give Infinity Elemental lifelink and it deals damage, you will gain infinite life. You might think infinite life sounds like a lot, and you’d be right. Once at infinite life, your life total mostly can’t change (see below). At infinite life and gain 2 life? You’re at infinite life. At infinite life and lose 29 life? You’re at infinite life. You can pay any amount of life and still be at infinite life. In fact, if you’re at infinite life and get with an opposing Infinity Elemental, you’d still be at infinite life.'}, {'date': '2020-02-29', 'text': 'If you’re at infinite life and attempt to draw a card from an empty library . . . you lose. You’re not immortal.'}, {'date': '2020-02-29', 'text': 'Infinity Elemental’s power is incalculable. Infinity Elemental has the greatest power among creatures on the battlefield (and off the battlefield for that matter), although it will tie with other Infinity Elementals. Gaining or losing power is meaningless to it, although effects that set its power to another number will still work.'}, {'date': '2020-02-29', 'text': 'Through various other cards, you can convert the infinite power of Infinity Elemental into other resources. For example, you could have infinite mana or create infinite tokens. You could draw infinite cards, but that many card don’t exist, so it’s probably not a great idea. Forcing another player to draw infinite cards, on the other hand . . . .'}]", "[{'date': '2020-02-29', 'text': 'If the die roll includes a physical component, such as Ol’ Buzzbark caring where the dice land, roll two dice for each one you would roll. You may need to use colored dice to keep each pair of dice clear so you can ignore one roll from each pair.'}, {'date': '2020-02-29', 'text': 'If you are instructed to roll multiple dice, roll two for each one and choose which one counts.'}, {'date': '2020-02-29', 'text': 'Krark’s Other Thumb applies to rerolls as well.'}, {'date': '2020-02-29', 'text': 'Krark’s Other Thumb works with any die you’re instructed or allowed to roll.'}, {'date': '2020-02-29', 'text': 'The ignored roll never happened as far as any other effect is concerned. For example, an ability that triggers “whenever you roll a die” won’t trigger for the ignored roll.'}]", "[{'date': '2020-02-29', 'text': 'If the only opponent who lost 3 life during the turn isn’t in the game at the beginning of that turn’s end step, the triggered ability will still trigger.'}, {'date': '2020-02-29', 'text': 'In a multiplayer game, the triggered ability will trigger no more than once a turn, even if multiple opponents lost 3 life during the turn.'}]", "[{'date': '2020-02-29', 'text': 'If the outside person says the story makes sense, nothing happens. The spell or ability will resolve (unless something else happens in response). If you control more than one Flavor Judge, or if you have a way to untap it, you can try again with a new outside person. While we can’t stop you from trying again with the same person, we encourage you to really think about whether that’s a good use of everyone’s time.'}, {'date': '2020-02-29', 'text': 'While players are discouraged from trying to overtly influence the outside person toward a desired answer, you may use tonal inflection, gestures, and the like to make your telling of the story more interesting. Simply explain what’s going to happen and let them decide if it makes sense.'}]", "[{'date': '2020-02-29', 'text': 'If you are playing with your head on the table, other players can’t attempt to physically dislodge you. They can try and talk you into moving.'}, {'date': '2020-02-29', 'text': 'If you’re not playing on a table, putting your head on the playing surface will count. (Wait, what are you playing on? Doesn’t matter.)'}, {'date': '2020-02-29', 'text': 'You can use any part of your head, such as your chin.'}, {'date': '2020-02-29', 'text': 'You don’t have to put your head on the table if you don’t want to. The enters-the-battlefield ability will still destroy the target creature, and Skull Saucer will then be sacrificed.'}]", "[{'date': '2020-02-29', 'text': 'If you choose 0 for the first number, you are allowed to choose -1 for the second. The same is true for ½ and -½.'}, {'date': '2020-02-29', 'text': 'Look at Me, I’m R&D applies to all numbers that appear on the card, including numbers in mana symbols, rules text, power, toughness, collector number, and art.'}, {'date': '2020-02-29', 'text': 'Note that you are changing numbers, not numerals within a number. Choosing 2 and 3 won’t change 20 to 30 or ½ to ⅓.'}, {'date': '2020-02-29', 'text': 'You can choose a number with a fraction.'}]", "[{'date': '2020-02-29', 'text': 'If you control more than one Bronze Calendar, speaking in one voice that isn’t your normal voice will satisfy the requirements of all of them.'}, {'date': '2020-02-29', 'text': 'You may change voices as often as you wish, as long as you avoid the one you use normally.'}]", "[{'date': '2020-02-29', 'text': 'If you create a token that’s a copy of Pointy Finger of Doom, spin whatever object you’re using to represent the token. Do not use living beings to represent the token.'}, {'date': '2020-02-29', 'text': 'Once the Finger starts spinning, no permanents can be moved until after the ability completely resolves.'}, {'date': '2020-02-29', 'text': 'Pointy Finger of Doom must rotate at least 360 degrees to count. There is no penalty for failing to spin it properly other than you must try again.'}, {'date': '2020-02-29', 'text': 'Pointy Finger of Doom won’t destroy itself, even though it is very close to itself.'}]", "[{'date': '2020-02-29', 'text': 'If you have multiple copies in your library while searching it, you may reveal all of them, provided you can pay the {1}{B} for each of them.'}, {'date': '2020-02-29', 'text': 'If you search your library multiple times in the same turn, each Infernal Spawn of Infernal Spawn of Evil in that library can be revealed once that turn. You may reveal multiples to prove you have multiples.'}, {'date': '2020-02-29', 'text': 'Infernal Spawn of Infernal Spawn of Evil doesn’t itself allow you to search your library. You must have another spell or ability that instructs you to search it.'}, {'date': '2020-02-29', 'text': 'You can reveal Infernal Spawn of Infernal Spawn of Evil any time while searching your library before you shuffle it.'}]", "[{'date': '2020-02-29', 'text': 'If your location doesn’t use a four-season system, you may need to get creative. Perhaps there is an analog you can use?'}, {'date': '2020-02-29', 'text': 'Ignore the weather when determining which season it is. For example, if it’s snowing in spring, it’s still spring.'}, {'date': '2020-02-29', 'text': 'To determine the season, use the calendar or whatever applies in your location.'}]", "[{'date': '2020-02-29', 'text': 'In a game with more than two players, players can use paper and pencil to track where their pop! counter is. That is, the tracker located in Water Gun Balloon Game’s art is fun, but not required to work.'}, {'date': '2020-02-29', 'text': 'Pink joins gold and every eye color imaginable in the set of colors in silver-bordered Magic games.'}, {'date': '2020-02-29', 'text': 'The pop! counters are considered to be on the inherent tracker in Water Gun Balloon Game’s art, not on the permanent itself. Those counters can’t be removed or moved to another permanent. Similarly, if you proliferate, you can’t add more pop! counters to Water Gun Balloon Game.'}]", "[{'date': '2020-02-29', 'text': 'Multiple Snickering Squirrels can be used on the same die roll. In this way, you can produce results that really aren’t normally possible, such as a result of 83 on a six-sided die.'}, {'date': '2020-02-29', 'text': 'Snickering Squirrel can be used to produce results that aren’t normally possible, such as a result of 7 on a six-sided die.'}, {'date': '2020-02-29', 'text': 'You can use Snickering Squirrel’s ability even if Snickering Squirrel came under your control this turn. (Note that it doesn’t use the {T} symbol.)'}, {'date': '2020-02-29', 'text': 'You decide whether to use Snickering Squirrel right after you see the result of the die roll.'}]", "[{'date': '2020-02-29', 'text': 'Mutate is a keyword from the future! Unless you’re reading this after it’s been released, in which case it’s … <spooky music>a keyword from the present!</spooky music>. But I suppose you could be reading this in a theoretical future in which the keyword was released, threatened to destroy Magic (as I do occasionally), and removed from the game, in which case it’s… <spookier music>a keyword from the past!</spookier music>.'}, {'date': '2020-02-29', 'text': 'Surgeon Commander’s color identity is all five colors, thanks to the mana symbols in its rules text. It can be included only in five-color Commander decks. Creative templating, indeed.'}]", "[{'date': '2020-02-29', 'text': 'Reveal is a keyword action. A card is revealed if a spell, ability, or rule instructed you to reveal it, using the word “reveal.” While you are allowed to show your hand to other players just because you feel like it, that isn’t “revealing” those cards.'}]", "[{'date': '2020-02-29', 'text': 'The mana produced by Underdome’s last ability can be spent to pay any cost imposed by a silver-bordered card. This includes casting a silver-bordered spell, activating the ability of a silver-bordered card, or paying for abilities like the one Infernal Spawn of Infernal Spawn of Evil has.'}]", "[{'date': '2020-02-29', 'text': 'The two die rolls are separate and sequential. If you want to reroll the first die, you must do so before you roll the second die.'}]", "[{'date': '2020-02-29', 'text': 'The word can include repeated letters.'}, {'date': '2020-02-29', 'text': 'The word should be in a language common to all players in the game.'}, {'date': '2020-02-29', 'text': 'While imitating a bell is sufficient if you’re the opponent, having a real bell with you in case your opponent has Rings a Bell in their deck will net you huge style points.'}]", "[{'date': '2020-02-29', 'text': 'The “Do nothing” on a result of 1 refers to that individual roll, not to all of Strategy, Schamtegy. For example, if you roll a 6 and then on the next roll you roll a 1, you’ll still roll again and process that result.'}]", "[{'date': '2020-02-29', 'text': 'You can open booster packs that contain cards not legal in the format you’re playing.'}, {'date': '2020-02-29', 'text': 'You can use any sealed Magic booster pack, including promo packs. As long as it hasn’t previously been opened and you don’t know the contents beforehand, you’re good.'}]", "[{'date': '2020-02-29', 'text': 'You do have to ask yourself permission to cast spells, although you can give yourself a one-time permission that applies to all future spells you cast.'}]", "[{'date': '2020-02-29', 'text': 'You must say the flavor text out loud or use a comparable method of communicating. For example, sign language is allowed, but if you sign the flavor text, you are considered to have spoken. Goblin Mime will notice.'}]", "[{'date': '2020-02-29', 'text': '“Doubles” means the results of each of the two rolls is identical, after applying any effects that may alter or affect the roll. For example, if you roll a 5 and a 6, and then use Snickering Squirrel to change the 5 into a 6, you’ve rolled doubles.'}]"]
foreign_names: []
printings: ['10E', '2ED', '2XM', '30A', '3ED', '40K', '4BB', '4ED', '5ED', '6ED', '7ED', '8ED', '9ED', 'AFR', 'AKH', 'AKR', 'ALA', 'ANA', 'ANB', 'ARC', 'ARN', 'ATH', 'AVR', 'BBD', 'BFZ', 'BLB', 'BRB', 'BRC', 'BRO', 'BTD', 'C13', 'C14', 'C15', 'C16', 'C17', 'C18', 'C19', 'CED', 'CEI', 'CHK', 'CLB', 'CLU', 'CM2', 'CMA', 'CMD', 'CMM', 'CMR', 'CST', 'DD1', 'DD2', 'DDC', 'DDD', 'DDE', 'DDF', 'DDG', 'DDH', 'DDI', 'DDJ', 'DDK', 'DDL', 'DDM', 'DDN', 'DDO', 'DDP', 'DDQ', 'DDR', 'DDS', 'DDT', 'DDU', 'DKM', 'DMR', 'DMU', 'DOM', 'DPA', 'DTK', 'DVD', 'E01', 'ELD', 'EVG', 'FBB', 'FRF', 'G17', 'GK1', 'GK2', 'GN2', 'GN3', 'GNT', 'GRN', 'GS1', 'GVL', 'H09', 'H17', 'HBG', 'HOP', 'HOU', 'ICE', 'IKO', 'INV', 'ISD', 'ITP', 'J14', 'J22', 'JMP', 'JVC', 'KHM', 'KLD', 'KLR', 'KTK', 'LCI', 'LEA', 'LEB', 'LRW', 'LTR', 'M10', 'M11', 'M12', 'M13', 'M14', 'M15', 'M19', 'M20', 'M21', 'MBS', 'MD1', 'ME1', 'ME3', 'MH2', 'MH3', 'MID', 'MIR', 'MKM', 'MMQ', 'MOM', 'MRD', 'NEO', 'NPH', 'ODY', 'OLEP', 'ONE', 'ONS', 'ORI', 'OTJ', 'P02', 'P23', 'P30M', 'PAL00', 'PAL01', 'PAL02', 'PAL03', 'PAL04', 'PAL05', 'PAL06', 'PAL99', 'PALP', 'PANA', 'PARL', 'PC2', 'PCA', 'PD2', 'PD3', 'PDGM', 'PELP', 'PF19', 'PF20', 'PGPX', 'PGRU', 'PHUK', 'PIP', 'PL24', 'PLST', 'PMPS', 'PMPS06', 'PMPS07', 'PMPS08', 'PMPS09', 'PMPS10', 'PMPS11', 'POR', 'PPP1', 'PRM', 'PRW2', 'PRWK', 'PS11', 'PSAL', 'PSS2', 'PSS3', 'PSS4', 'PTC', 'PTK', 'PZ2', 'RAV', 'REX', 'RIX', 'RNA', 'ROE', 'RQS', 'RTR', 'S99', 'SCD', 'SHM', 'SIR', 'SLD', 'SNC', 'SOI', 'SOM', 'STX', 'SUM', 'TD0', 'TD2', 'THB', 'THS', 'TMP', 'TPR', 'TSP', 'UGL', 'ULST', 'UND', 'UNF', 'UNH', 'USG', 'UST', 'VOW', 'WAR', 'WC00', 'WC01', 'WC02', 'WC03', 'WC04', 'WC97', 'WC98', 'WC99', 'WHO', 'WOE', 'XANA', 'XLN', 'ZEN', 'ZNR']
original_text: ['"Until end of turn" and "this turn" effects don\'t end.', '(This creature has INFINITE POWER.)', "All creatures have haste.\\nPlayers play with the top card of their libraries revealed.\\nNoninstant, nonsorcery cards on top of a library are on the battlefield under their owner's control in addition to being in that library.", "As B.\\nO.\\nB. enters the battlefield, create four 1/1 blue Beeble creature tokens.\\nThe number of loyalty counters on B.\\nO.\\nB. is equal to the number of Beebles you control. (Create or sacrifice Beebles whenever B.\\nO.\\nB. gains or loses loyalty.)\\n+1: Up to X target Beebles can't be blocked this turn, where X is the number of cards in your hand.\\n−1: Draw a card.", 'As Boomstacker enters the battlefield and whenever it attacks, stack two dice on top of it. (All dice must be stacked vertically, one on top of another.)\\nBoomstacker gets +1/+1 for each die in its stack.\\nBoomstacker attacks each combat if able.\\nWhen the stack falls, sacrifice Boomstacker.', 'As Elvish Impersonators enters the battlefield, roll a six-sided die twice. Its base power becomes the first result and its base toughness becomes the second result.', "As Look at Me, I'm R&D comes into play, choose a number and a second number one higher or one lower than that number.\\nAll instances of the first chosen number on permanents, spells, and cards in any zone are the second chosen number.", 'As Rings a Bell enters the battlefield, choose a word with four or more letters.\\nAfter you say the chosen word for the first time each turn, an opponent may ring or imitate a bell within five seconds. When no opponent does, draw a card.', 'As Six-y Beast enters the battlefield, you secretly put six or fewer +1/+1 counters on it, then an opponent guesses the number of counters. If that player guesses right, sacrifice Six-y Beast after it enters the battlefield.', 'As Water Gun Balloon Game enters the battlefield, each player puts a pop counter on "0."\\nWhenever a player casts a spell, move that player\'s pop counter up one.\\nWhenever a player\'s pop counter hits "5," that player creates a 5/5 pink Giant Teddy Bear creature token and resets all pop counters to "0."', 'At the beginning of your upkeep, roll a six-sided die. On a 3 or 4, put a +1/+1 counter on Inhumaniac. On a 5 or higher, put two +1/+1 counters on it. On a 1, remove all +1/+1 counters from Inhumaniac.', 'At the beginning of your upkeep, roll a six-sided die. Painiac gets +X/+0 until end of turn, where X is the result.', 'At the beginning of your upkeep, you may ask target player a yes-or-no question. If you do, that player answers the question truthfully and abides by that answer if able until end of turn.', 'B', 'Ban a card other than a basic land card for the rest of the match. (All cards with that name in any zone or sideboard are removed from the match.)', "Creatures named Paper Tiger can't attack or block.", "Creatures named Rock Lobster can't attack or block.", "Creatures named Scissors Lizard can't attack or block.", 'Defender\\nYou may tap an untapped Wall you control to have any player reroll a die that player rolled.', 'Destroy target creature with reminder text. (Reminder text is any italicized text in parentheses that explains rules you already know.)', "During your turn, if Entirely Normal Armchair is in your hand, you may hide it on the battlefield.\\n{0}: Return Entirely Normal Armchair to its owner's hand. Only any opponent may activate this ability and only if they see Entirely Normal Armchair.\\n{2}, Sacrifice Entirely Normal Armchair: Destroy target attacking creature.", 'Enchant creature\\nAs Magic Word enters the battlefield, choose a word.\\nWhisper the chosen word: Tap enchanted creature.', 'Equipped creature gets +2/+2 and has protection from Rogues and from Clerics.\\nWhenever equipped creature deals combat damage to a player, create a 4/4 gold Dragon creature token with flying and roll a d20 (a twenty-sided die). If you roll a 20, repeat this process.\\nEquip {2}', 'Exile target attacking creature. Then remove it from the game. Then put it into the absolutely-removed-from-the-freaking-game-forever zone.', 'First strike\\n{1}{W}, Do the Hokey Pokey: Prevent all damage a source of your choice would deal to Knight of the Hokey Pokey this turn. (To do the Hokey Pokey, choose an arm, a leg, or your whole self and put it in. Put it out. Put it in. Shake it all about. You do the Hokey Pokey Turn yourself around.)', 'Flying, first strike, trample, haste\\nThis spell costs {3} less to cast for each card you\'ve revealed this turn.\\nWhen Infernius Spawnington III, Esq. enters the battlefield, you may say "I\'m here." If you do, it deals 3 damage to target player.', 'Flying, first strike, trample\\nOnce each turn, while you\'re searching your library, you may pay {1}{B}, reveal Infernal Spawn of Infernal Spawn of Evil from your library and say "I\'m coming, too" If you do, Infernal Spawn of Infernal Spawn of Evil deals 2 damage to a player of your choice.', 'Flying, first strike\\n{1}{B}, Reveal Infernal Spawn of Evil from your hand, Say "It\'s coming": Infernal Spawn of Evil deals 1 damage to target opponent or planeswalker. Activate this ability only during your upkeep and only once each turn.', 'Flying\\nAs Jumbo Imp enters the battlefield, roll a six-sided die. Jumbo Imp enters the battlefield with a number of +1/+1 counters on it equal to the result.\\nAt the beginning of your upkeep, roll a six-sided die and put a number of +1/+1 counters on Jumbo Imp equal to the result.\\nAt the beginning of your end step, roll a six-sided die and remove a number of +1/+1 counters from Jumbo Imp equal to the result.', 'Flying\\nAt the beginning of each end step, if an opponent lost 3 or more life this turn,\\nAugment {1}{B} ({1}{B}, Reveal this card from your hand: Combine it with target host. Augment only as a sorcery.)', 'Flying\\nAt the beginning of your upkeep, sacrifice Carnivorous Death-Parrot unless you say its flavor text.', 'Flying\\nWhen Skull Saucer enters the battlefield, destroy target creature and put your head on the table. Sacrifice Skull Saucer when your head stops touching the table.', "Flying\\nWhenever Stet, Draconic Proofreader attacks, you may exile a card from your graveyard. When you do, Stet, Draconic Proofreader deals 4 damage to any target whose name begins with the same letter as the exiled card.\\n{W}: Delete the first letter of target permanent or player's name until end of turn.", 'Flying\\nWhenever a creature dies, you may roll a six-sided die. If you roll a 1, sacrifice Poultrygeist. Otherwise, put a +1/+1 counter on Poultrygeist.', 'Flying\\nWhenever you attack with two or more creatures,\\nAugment {3}{W} ({3}{W}, Reveal this card from your hand: Combine it with target host. Augment only as a sorcery.)', 'G', "Hoisted Hireling has flying as long as it's being held above the battlefield.", 'If Cheatyface is in your hand, you may sneak Cheatyface onto the battlefield. If an opponent catches you right away, that player may exile Cheatyface.\\nFlying', 'If you would roll a die, instead roll two of those dice and ignore one of those results.', "Just a second (As long as this spell is on the stack, players can't move cards on the battlefield.)\\nSlaying Mantis enters the battlefield by being thrown from a distance of at least three feet.\\nWhen Slaying Mantis enters the battlefield, it fights each creature an opponent controls that it touched as it entered.", 'Knightlifelink (Damage dealt by Knights you control also causes you to gain that much life.)\\n{W}: Syr Cadian, Knight Owl gains vigilance until end of turn. Activate this ability only from sunrise to sunset.\\n{B}: Syr Cadian, Knight Owl gains flying until end of turn. Activate this ability only from sunset to sunrise.', 'Madness {R}, cycling {1}{R}, kicker {2}{R}, flashback {3}{R}, buyback {4}{R}\\nBlast from the Past deals 2 damage to any target. If this spell was kicked, create a 1/1 red Goblin creature token.', "Menace (This creature can't be blocked except by two or more creatures.)\\nOther creatures you control wearing hats in their art have menace.", 'Open a sealed Magic booster pack, reveal the cards, and put one of them into your hand. (Remove that card from your deck before beginning a new game.)', 'Phasing, cumulative upkeep {1}, echo, fading 3, bands with other Dinosaurs, protection from Homarids, snow-covered plainswalk, flanking, rampage 2', 'Players play a Magic subgame under the table, starting at 5 life and using their libraries as their decks. The winner searches their library for two cards, puts those cards into their hand, then shuffles their library.', 'R', 'Roll a six-sided die. If you roll a 1, Goblin Tutor has no effect. Otherwise, search your library for the indicated card, reveal it, put it into your hand, then shuffle your library.\\n2 — A card named Goblin Tutor\\n3 — An enchantment\\n4 — An artifact\\n5 — A creature\\n6 — An instant or sorcery', "Roll a six-sided die. Put target nonland permanent into its owner's library just beneath the top X cards of that library, where X is the result.", 'Roll a six-sided die. Strategy, Schmategy has the indicated effect.\\n1 — Do nothing.\\n2 — Destroy all artifacts.\\n3 — Destroy all lands.\\n4 — Strategy, Schmategy deals 3 damage to each creature and each player.\\n5 — Each player discards their hand and draws seven cards.\\n6 — Repeat this process two more times.', 'Roll a six-sided die. Target creature gets +X/+X until end of turn, where X is the result.', 'Say "Goblin S.W.A.T. Team": Put a +1/+1 counter on Goblin S.W.A.T. Team unless an opponent swats the table within five seconds. Activate this ability only once each turn.', 'Spells you cast cost {1} less to cast.\\nYou must speak in a voice other than your normal voice.\\nWhen you speak in your normal voice, sacrifice Bronze Calendar.', "The phases of each player's turn are reversed. (The phases are, in reverse order, ending, postcombat main, combat, precombat main, and beginning.)\\nAs long as there are more than two players in the game, the turn order is reversed.", "This spell costs {1} more to cast for each ten years you've been alive.\\nAvatar of Me's power is equal to your height in feet and its toughness is equal to your American shoe size. Round to the nearest ½.\\nAvatar of Me is the color of your eyes.", "Trample (This spell can deal excess damage to its target's controller.)\\nSuper-Duper Death Ray deals 4 damage to target creature.", 'Trample\\nWhenever a player casts a spell, put a chip counter on its converted mana cost.\\nB-I-N-G-O gets +9/+9 for each set of three numbers in a row with chip counters on them.', 'U', 'W', "When GO TO JAIL enters the battlefield, exile target creature an opponent controls until GO TO JAIL leaves the battlefield.\\nAt the beginning of the upkeep of the exiled card's owner, that player rolls two six-sided dice. If they roll doubles, sacrifice GO TO JAIL.", "When Spirit of the Season enters the battlefield, it gains haste if it's summer. Put a +1/+1 counter on it if it's autumn. You gain 5 life if it's winter. If it's spring, search your library for a basic land card, reveal it, put it into your hand, then shuffle your library.", 'When this creature enters the battlefield,\\nexile target creature card with converted mana cost 2 or less from your graveyard. If it has augment, combine it with a host you control. Otherwise, put it onto the battlefield.', 'When this creature enters the battlefield,\\nroll a six-sided die. Put a number of +1/+1 counters on this creature equal to the result.', 'When this creature enters the battlefield,\\nroll a six-sided die. You gain life equal to the result.', 'When this creature enters the battlefield,\\nsearch your library for a basic land card, reveal it, put it into your hand, then shuffle your library.', 'When this creature enters the battlefield,\\ntarget creature an opponent controls gets -1/-1 until end of turn.', 'When this creature enters the battlefield,\\ntarget opponent discards a card.', 'When this creature enters the battlefield,\\nyou may discard a card. If you do, draw a card.', 'When this creature enters the battlefield,\\nyou may draw a card.', "When this creature enters the battlefield,\\nyou may exile target non-Horse creature you control that wasn't put onto the battlefield with this ability this turn, then return it to the battlefield under its owner's control.", 'Whenever a 6 is rolled on a six-sided die, put a +1/+1 counter on each Bird. (You may roll dice only when instructed to.)\\nTap an untapped Bird you control: Roll a six-sided die. (Like now.)', 'Whenever a nontoken creature enters the battlefield,\\nAugment {G} ({G}, Reveal this card from your hand: Combine it with target host. Augment only as a sorcery.)', 'Whenever a player casts a spell without asking your permission while casting it, counter that spell.\\nWhen a player asks you permission to cast a spell and you refuse, counter that spell and sacrifice Common Courtesy.', 'Whenever another creature enters the battlefield, you may stand up and say in a deep, booming voice, "Presenting . . . " and that creature\'s name. If you do, put a +1/+1 counter on that creature.', 'Whenever you augment, enchant, or mutate a creature you control, draw a card.\\n{T}: Add {W}, {U}, {B}, {R}, or {G}.', 'Whenever you cast a spell with a squirrel in its art, you get (an acorn counter).\\nWhenever a Squirrel you control enters the battlefield or dies, you get .\\n{2}{B}, Pay X : Target creature gets -X/-X until end of turn.\\n{G}, Pay X : Target creature gets +X/+X until end of turn.', "Whenever you cast a spell, note the first letter of its artist's name. If that letter wasn't already noted, put a +1/+1 counter on Abstract Iguanart.", "Whenever you cast a wordy spell, scry 2. (A spell is wordy if it has four or more lines of rules text.)\\n{1}{R}, {T}: Choose target creature you don't control. Reveal the top card of your library. Alexander Clamilton gets +X/+0 until end of turn, where X is the number of lines of rules text of the revealed card. Alexander Clamilton fights that creature.", 'Who\\no\\nXo\\nW\\nInstant\\nTarget player gains X life.\\n//\\nWhat\\no2o\\nR\\nInstant\\nDestroy target artifact.\\n//\\nWhen\\no2o\\nU\\nInstant\\nCounter target creature spell.\\n//\\nWhere\\no3o\\nB\\nInstant\\nDestroy target land.\\n//\\nWhy\\no1o\\nG\\nInstant\\nDestroy target enchantment.', "You may play cards as though they were other cards of your choice with the same mana cost. You can't choose the same card twice. (Mana cost includes color.)", 'You may tap Snickering Squirrel to increase the result of a die any player rolled by 1.', '{1}{G}: Reveal a card in your hand, covering the artist credit. Target opponent guesses the artist. If they guess wrong, create a 1/1 green Squirrel creature token.', '{1}{G}: Roll two six-sided dice. If both results are the same, Free-Range Chicken gets +X/+X until end of turn, where X is that result. If the total of those results is equal to any other total you have rolled this turn for this ability, sacrifice it. (For example, if you roll two 3s, it gets +3/+3. If you roll a 4 and a 2 for this ability later that turn, sacrifice it.)', '{2}{G}, {T}: Roll a six-sided die. It becomes a green Die creature token with power and toughness each equal to its result.\\n{2}{U}, {T}: Reroll any die. (Activate this ability only any time it makes sense.)', '{3}, {T}: Spin Pointy Finger of Doom in the middle of the table so that it rotates completely at least once, then destroy the closest permanent the finger points to.', '{4}: Search your library for a card, put that card into your hand, then shuffle your library.', '{4}: You may put a creature card from your hand onto the battlefield.', '{T}: Add {C}.\\n{T}: Add one mana of any color. Spend this mana only to pay silver-bordered costs.', '{T}: Choose target spell or ability that targets a permanent you control. Then ask a person outside the game if the story of what will happen makes sense. If they say no, sacrifice Flavor Judge and counter that spell or ability.', '{T}: Roll a six-sided die. This ability has the indicated effect.\\n1 — Sacrifice Jack-in-the-Mox and you lose 5 life.\\n2 — Add {W}.\\n3 — Add {U}.\\n4 — Add {B}.\\n5 — Add {R}.\\n6 — Add {G}.', '{W}, {T}: Tap target creature without reminder text. (Reminder text is any italicized text in parentheses that explains rules you already know.)']
